ished_074_germanic_languages_pt01_scandinavian_loanword_doublets", "label": "Native/Scandinavian-loan doublets (shirt/skirt, yard/garden)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "p.118", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_strong_weak_verb_distinction", "label": "OE strong/weak verb classes (ablaut vs. dental-suffix past)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.123-124", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_preterite_present_verbs", "label": "Preterite-present (premodal) verbs", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "p.124", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_reflexive_self_development", "label": "OE bare-pronoun reflexive to emphatic -self development", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "p.127", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_v2_particle_placement", "label": "OE Verb-Second and particle-placement asymmetry", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.396-458", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_case_to_word_order_shift", "label": "OE case-marking collapse to ME word-order/preposition reanalysis", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.406-417", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_quirky_subject_lician", "label": "Quirky dative subject (lician 'like')", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.419-425", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Even in Old English the nominative subject is usually the reflexive antecedent and agreement trigger, but the chapter traces an exception via lician: a non-nominative dative Experiencer can behave syntactically like a subject while a true nominative exists elsewhere in the clause, paralleling Icelandic quirky subjects; reinterpreted as true nominative only in the 15th century."}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_norman_viking_contact", "label": "Viking and Norman-Conquest contact shaping English", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.166-222", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Viking settlement/Danelaw and the 1066 Norman Conquest are argued as the two successive invasion-contact events that reshaped English lexically and (for the Conquest, more weakly than conventionally assumed) morphologically; the author argues unstressed-syllable phonological reduction driving case-ending loss was already underway in the north before French contact, favoring a Viking-settlement explanation over the traditional French-contact one for the morphological changes specifically."}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_oe_passive", "label": "OE/ME passive: impersonal dative vs. subject-promoting passivization", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/074_germanic_languages_pt01.md", "source_location": "pp.427-435", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_074_germanic_languages_pt01_negation_ne_naht", "label": "OE preverbal ne to